        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "36b56088-a50e-56b5-803c-f2a424bb4a59",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jefferson Davis; Alexander H. Stephens; Franklin Pierce\n- Organizations: Confederate States of America; American Civil War Museum\n- Places: White House of the Confederacy; Richmond; Appomattox\n- Roles: President of the Confederate States; Vice President of the Confederate States; United States Secretary of War\n- Events: Confederate States presidential election, 1861\n\nFacts:\n- The White House of the Confederacy was built in 1818.\n- Jefferson Davis served as the 23rd United States Secretary of War under President Franklin Pierce from 1853 to 1857.\n- In the 1861 Confederate States presidential election, Jefferson Davis and Alexander H. Stephens, who had already been elected under the Provisional Confederate States Constitution, were elected as the first permanent President and Vice President of the Confederate States of America.\n- Jefferson Davis and Alexander H. Stephens served six-year terms from February 22, 1862, to February 22, 1868.\n- The White House of the Confederacy was the main executive residence of Confederate President Jefferson Davis from August 1861 until April 1865.\n- The American Civil War Museum operates three sites: the Museum and White House of the Confederacy, the American Civil War Center at Historic Tredegar in Richmond, and the Museum of the Confederacy–Appomattox.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "473d13e6-d9c3-5985-ad9b-5900a145ec5d",
        "text": "Page: Confederate States presidential election, 1861. Sentence: Jefferson Davis, who had been elected President and Alexander H. Stephens, who had been elected Vice President, under the Provisional Confederate States Constitution, were elected to six-year terms (February 22, 1862 – February 22, 1868) as the first permanent President and Vice President of the Confederate States of America.\nPage: Jefferson Davis. Sentence: He was the 23rd United States Secretary of War, serving under U.S. President Franklin Pierce from 1853 to 1857.\nPage: White House of the Confederacy. Sentence: Built in 1818, it was the main executive residence of the sole President of the Confederate States of America, Jefferson Davis, from August 1861 until April 1865.\nPage: American Civil War Museum. Sentence: The museum operates three sites: The Museum and White House of the Confederacy and the American Civil War Center at Historic Tredegar in Richmond, and the Museum of the Confederacy–Appomattox.",
